zend_qsort.c: swap pointer-sized units when the elements allow it, so pointers keep their tag.

zend_hash_sort builds a Bucket ** array and hands it to zend_qsort, whose _zend_qsort_swap
moves an element as ints and then as chars. On Capstone a pointer copied through int or
char stores comes back without its capability tag. For a 20-element array zend_qsort swaps
slots 0 and 9 before the first comparison, so array_data_compare (sort), array_key_compare
(ksort) and array_user_compare (usort) dereference an untagged Bucket * at their first
`f->pData` or `f->nKeyLength` and fault with cause 24 -- on any input, with or without a
defect in the program. Measured 2026-10-27 on the virtual profile: `sort(array(3,1,2))`
faults; a 20-line probe swapping two malloc'd pointers through int stores, then char
stores, leaves both tags clear.

The collaborator's recorded result read exactly this fault as a catch: CRASH-022 (usort,
ext/standard/array.c:546) CAUGHT on all four arms and CRASH-078 (array_diff_assoc's key sort, array.c:162) the
same, every cell cause 24, none cause 25. Those eight cells are this swap, not the defect.

Same rule as the PostgreSQL port's 0013 for sort_template.h: an element that holds a
pointer is pointer-aligned and its size is a multiple of the pointer size, so when both
element addresses and the size allow it the swap moves void * units, which the compiler
copies as capabilities; anything else keeps the int and char loops exactly as they were.
Guarded by __SIZEOF_INTCAP__, which only the capability compiler defines, so a native build
of the same tree is byte-for-byte the release's. gnu89: the block sits after the function's
declarations, and the loop variable is declared with them.

Applied to the prepared source tree by prepare-sources.sh, for every arm: a stock arm that
cannot sort is not a stock arm.

--- a/Zend/zend_qsort.c
+++ b/Zend/zend_qsort.c
@@ -33,6 +33,26 @@
 	register size_t i;
 	int             t_i;
 	char            t_c;
+#if defined(__SIZEOF_INTCAP__)
+	/* capstone: a pointer moved as ints or chars loses its capability tag, and
+	 * zend_hash_sort sorts an array of Bucket *. An element that holds a pointer is
+	 * pointer-aligned and a multiple of the pointer size, so swap in pointer-sized
+	 * units whenever both elements and the size allow it; the compiler copies those
+	 * as capabilities. Anything else keeps the loops below. */
+	if (siz % sizeof(void *) == 0 &&
+	    ((size_t) a) % sizeof(void *) == 0 &&
+	    ((size_t) b) % sizeof(void *) == 0) {
+		void **pa = (void **) a;
+		void **pb = (void **) b;
+		size_t n;
+		for (n = 0; n < siz / sizeof(void *); n++) {
+			void *t = pa[n];
+			pa[n] = pb[n];
+			pb[n] = t;
+		}
+		return;
+	}
+#endif
 
 	tmp_a_int = (int *) a;
 	tmp_b_int = (int *) b;
